   "claims": [
    "The Difference Between Random and Non-Random Sequences Consider the following sequence of numbers: 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 , 5.",
    "Because after the number 5, the subsequent number, 6, can be predicted with near absolute certainty.",
    "For example, when you roll a fair six-sided die for the first time, any number from 1 to 6 has an equal probability of landing face up.",
    "For example, if we use the following poorly chosen parameter set: X₀ = 3, a = 4, c = 5, m = 6 we end up generating a highly repetitive, short-looped sequence of 3, 5, 1, 3, 5, 1 which clearly does not look random at all.",
    "Generators relying on linear congruential methods were first successfully cracked by Jim Reeds in 1977, and subsequently by Joan Boyar in 1982 (who also managed to compromise quadratic and cubic generators).",
    "Modern Hardware Random Number Generators QRBG121 The QRBG121 utilizes true quantum randomness."
